  "randomnessSheetEyebrow": "What lies behind chance",
  "randomnessSheetTitle": "PRNGs, secure RNGs & physical entropy",
  "randomnessCard1Title": "Three related but different sources",
  "randomnessCard1Body": "A PRNG deterministically expands a seed and internal state; it is useful for games and simulations but is not designed to resist prediction. A CSPRNG (often built as a DRBG) is designed to make outputs hard to predict, usually using operating-system entropy. A physical entropy source measures a physical process. These categories are related, not interchangeable.",
  "randomnessCard2Title": "What this app uses",
  "randomnessCard2Body": "When available, the app requests integers from RANDOM.ORG, which documents atmospheric radio noise as an entropy source. Its output can be conditioned and buffered before a request; we do not measure or verify the provider's process ourselves. Offline, the app uses Dart Random.secure(), an OS-backed CSPRNG when supported. If secure randomness is unsupported, the app falls back to an ordinary PRNG and labels it as non-cryptographic.",
  "randomnessCard3Title": "Entropy is not proof",
  "randomnessCard3Body": "Entropy describes uncertainty, not a proof that a particular result is unbiased or independently verifiable. Conditioning and hashing can remove bias but cannot create missing entropy; statistical tests can find some problems but cannot prove randomness. Secure generators do not need fresh physical noise for every value. For a six-sided die, use a bounded integer API (such as nextInt(6) + 1) to avoid modulo bias. A plain remainder operation can favor some faces when the range does not divide evenly; bounded APIs avoid that modulo bias.",
  "randomnessSheetButton": "Got it",
  "randomOrgFallbackNotice": "RANDOM.ORG unavailable. Using local secure RNG (CSPRNG).",
  "secureRandomUnavailableNotice": "RANDOM.ORG unavailable and secure RNG unsupported. Using local PRNG (not cryptographically secure)."
